Closed-Loop Neuroadaptive Civilizational Simulation

## CLNCS — Version 0.3

### *Measuring the Persistence of Human Cognitive Structure in Artificial Civilizations*

---

## Abstract

We propose a single, operationally defined scientific question:

> **Does a continuous stream of low-bandwidth neurophysiological signals — decoded in real time from non-invasive EEG — constitute a measurable selective pressure on the long-term trajectory of an artificial civilization populated by autonomous AI agents?**

The primary instrument for answering this question is the **Neural Imprint**, defined as the Transfer Entropy from the human's decoded cognitive state history to the civilizational state graph:

$$\mathcal{N}(t) = \text{TE}(H \to \mathcal{C}) = H\bigl(\mathcal{C}_{t+\tau} \mid \mathcal{C}_{0:t}\bigr) - H\bigl(\mathcal{C}_{t+\tau} \mid \mathcal{C}_{0:t},\ H_{0:t}\bigr)$$

$\mathcal{N}(t) > 0$ means the human's past cognitive state reduces uncertainty about the civilization's future beyond what the civilization's own history predicts. That is the precise, formal definition of *the simulation remembers the human.*

Everything else in this document is infrastructure for measuring $\mathcal{N}(t)$ reliably.

