## 1. The Central Claim, Precisely Stated

Version 0.1 asked whether EEG could control a virtual world. That is engineering.

Version 0.2 asked whether a human's mind leaves traces in a civilization. That is science, but imprecisely stated.

Version 0.3 asks one question with mathematical precision:

$$\text{Does } \mathcal{N}(t) = \text{TE}(H_{0:t} \to \mathcal{C}_t) \text{ grow over time in the neuroadaptive condition and converge near zero in controls?}$$

Transfer Entropy is an information-theoretic measure that quantifies the directional transfer of information between two time series — specifically the reduction in uncertainty about the future state of one variable when the past of another is known, beyond what that variable's own history already provides. [PubMed Central](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11854896/) It is not correlation. It is not mutual information. It is directed, causal, and asymmetric — exactly what we need to answer whether the human *shaped* the civilization rather than merely *correlated* with it.

Transfer entropy can sometimes fail to identify existing causal connections and sometimes suggest a connection where none exists, depending on the cognitive task and system architecture. [nih](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7516857/) We take this seriously. This is why estimator convergence across multiple methods is required, not optional (see Section 6).

