## 2. Formal Definitions — The Complete System

### 2.1 The Mental State Vector

$$\mathbf{M}_t = (C_t,\ L_t,\ E_t,\ A_t,\ F_t) \in [0,1]^5$$

- $C_t$ = concentration: frontal $\beta/\alpha$ ratio
- $L_t$ = cognitive load: frontal-midline $\theta$ elevation, $\alpha$ suppression
- $E_t$ = engagement: $P_\beta / (P_\alpha + P_\theta)$
- $A_t$ = affective valence: frontal $\alpha$ asymmetry (left minus right)
- $F_t$ = fatigue: $\delta$ rise over time, collapse of $\alpha$ baseline

**Note on curiosity.** Curiosity is excluded from $\mathbf{M}_t$ because EEG does not directly measure it. It is instead a latent variable reconstructed from joint neural and behavioral evidence:

$$\kappa_t = f(\mathbf{M}_t,\ \Delta\text{ExplorationRadius}_t,\ \text{AgentInitiation}_t,\ \text{DwellNovelty}_t)$$

This is more defensible, more interesting, and opens a secondary research question: *is curiosity better predicted by the brain or by the body in motion?*

### 2.2 The Civilization as an Instrumented Graph

We formalize civilization as a time-evolving attributed graph:

$$\mathcal{G}_t = (V_t,\ E_t,\ \phi_t,\ \psi_t)$$

where:
- $V_t$ = agent nodes (each with memory state, role, skill vector, resource inventory)
- $E_t$ = directed edges encoding social interactions (trade, communication, alliance, conflict)
- $\phi_t: V \to \mathbb{R}^d$ = node attribute function (agent state embedding)
- $\psi_t: E \to \mathbb{R}^k$ = edge attribute function (interaction type, frequency, recency)

From $\mathcal{G}_t$ we extract **six measurable graph observables:**

| Observable | Symbol | Definition |
|---|---|---|
| Clustering coefficient | $\gamma_t$ | Mean local connectivity density |
| Modularity | $Q_t$ | Separation of agent subgroups |
| Hierarchy depth | $\delta_t$ | Longest chain of authority/influence |
| Institution density | $\iota_t$ | Fraction of edges encoding norm-governed interactions |
| Information diffusion rate | $\rho_t$ | Mean time for a signal to cross the graph |
| Knowledge entropy | $\mathcal{H}(K_t)$ | Shannon entropy of the knowledge stock distribution |

Research on LLM-agent communities already demonstrates that clustering, modularity, and value-aligned social partitions emerge naturally in multi-agent populations without explicit programming — and that these graph-level properties are empirically measurable. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/html/2512.10665v1)

The civilizational state vector used in the Neural Imprint computation is:

$$\mathcal{C}_t = (\gamma_t,\ Q_t,\ \delta_t,\ \iota_t,\ \rho_t,\ \mathcal{H}(K_t)) \in \mathbb{R}^6$$

Six dimensions. All computable. All interpretable.

### 2.3 Civilizational Fitness — Pareto, Not Weighted Sum

Version 0.2 used a weighted fitness function $\Phi_\mathcal{C} = \sum w_i X_i$. A reviewer would immediately ask: why those weights? No good answer exists. So we eliminate the question by eliminating the weights.

We define civilizational performance as a **multi-objective vector:**

$$\mathbf{F}_\mathcal{C} = (K,\ T,\ \mathcal{I},\ S) \in \mathbb{R}^4$$

knowledge, technology, institutions, stability — and we analyze the **Pareto frontier** of outcomes across experimental conditions. An outcome is Pareto-efficient if no individual objective can be improved without degrading another. The set of all such outcomes defines the Pareto frontier — a rigorous, assumption-free way to characterize multi-dimensional performance without imposing arbitrary trade-offs. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/pareto-frontier)

A civilization in the neuroadaptive condition that occupies a different region of the Pareto frontier than controls has been genuinely shaped by its cognitive climate. The frontier is the finding.

