## 4. Timescale Separation — Formal

Three timescales govern CLNCS. They must not be mixed.

$$\Delta t_f = 100\text{ ms} \quad \text{(EEG feature extraction)}$$
$$\Delta t_m = 1\text{–}10\text{ s} \quad \text{(agent decision cycle)}$$
$$\Delta t_s = 1\text{–}7\text{ days} \quad \text{(institutional evolution, graph topology change)}$$

The neural signal does not command agents in real time. The correct model:

$$\mathbf{M}_{t} \xrightarrow{\text{modulates}} \boldsymbol{\lambda}_t \xrightarrow{\text{biases}} \mathcal{P}(\text{agent action}) \xrightarrow{\text{accumulates over } \Delta t_s} \Delta\mathcal{G}_t$$

The human's concentration at 3:47pm on Tuesday does not determine which agent becomes a lawgiver. Over thirty days, a sustained pattern of high concentration and low stress biases the probability landscape toward exploratory and innovative agent behavior, which accumulates into a statistically distinct graph topology. The human is a climate, not a keyboard.

