## 5. The Neural Imprint — Full Estimation Protocol

This is the most technically vulnerable section. We treat it accordingly.

### 5.1 Why Transfer Entropy, Not Raw Mutual Information

Raw mutual information $\mathcal{I}(H; \mathcal{C})$ captures association. Transfer entropy $\text{TE}(H \to \mathcal{C})$ captures directed, lagged influence — exactly the claim we are making. The distinction matters enormously: a civilization that merely reflects the human (mirror) produces high MI; a civilization that is *shaped by* the human (imprint) produces high TE. We want imprint, not mirror.

### 5.2 Estimator Convergence Requirement

MI estimation in high-dimensional evolving systems is notoriously noisy and estimator-dependent. We require **convergence across four independent estimators:**

| Estimator | Strength | Weakness |
|---|---|---|
| **Kraskov k-NN** | Non-parametric, reliable for continuous data | Slow for very high dimensions |
| **MINE** (neural MI estimation) | Scales to high dimensions | Requires training, may overfit |
| **Transfer Entropy** (symbolic) | Robust to noise | Requires discretization |
| **Directed Information** (Markov) | Captures sequential causality | Assumes Markov structure |

A Neural Imprint result is reported only when all four estimators agree in sign and approximate magnitude. Divergence is itself a finding — it suggests the coupling is highly nonlinear or that the civilization graph has structural properties that defeat particular estimators.

We use the **Java Information Dynamics Toolkit (JIDT)** for TE and symbolic estimators, **MINE** for the neural estimator, and **the Python Optimal Transport (POT) library** for Schrödinger Bridge computations.

### 5.3 The Dimensionality Problem

$\mathcal{C}_t \in \mathbb{R}^6$ and $H_{0:t}$ is a long time series. Direct TE estimation may fail in high dimensions. We address this with a two-stage approach:

**Stage 1:** Reduce $\mathcal{C}_t$ to its two principal components via PCA across all conditions. Compute TE on the reduced representation for interpretability.

**Stage 2:** Compute TE separately for each of the six $\mathcal{C}$ dimensions against the full $H_{0:t}$. This reveals *which civilizational dimension* the human most strongly imprints — institutions, knowledge stock, technological trajectory, or social topology.

