## 8. Anticipated Objections and Responses

A committee will arrive with three weapons. Here they are, and here is why none of them kills the proposal.

**Objection 1 (neuroscience reviewer): "EEG signals are too noisy."**
Correct. Individual EEG samples are highly noisy. But $\mathcal{N}(t)$ is computed over thousands of samples across days. Statistical power accumulates precisely because we are measuring integrated influence, not moment-to-moment accuracy. Climate science does not require perfect temperature measurements; it requires reliable long-run averages. Same principle.

**Objection 2 (complex systems reviewer): "Civilization metrics are arbitrary."**
Partially correct. The fitness vector $\mathbf{F}_\mathcal{C}$ components are individually arguable. But we analyze Pareto frontiers, not scalar scores, which removes the arbitrariness from the comparison. And the six graph observables ($\gamma, Q, \delta, \iota, \rho, \mathcal{H}$) are standard network science measures with well-established interpretations. Research on LLM-agent social networks already demonstrates that clustering, modularity, and tie persistence aggregate from micro-level agent signals into macro-level network structures that can be measured with standard graph-theoretic tools. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.19299)

**Objection 3 (AI reviewer): "Agent behavior depends more on prompt engineering than on EEG."**
True — and by design. The coupling $W$ is weak relative to internal agent dynamics. This is the climate model. A climate does not overpower internal ecosystem dynamics; it biases their probability distribution. If the objection were valid, $\mathcal{N}(t)$ would be zero. The experiment tests that directly.

