## 9. The Contribution Hierarchy

Let us be precise about novelty at each level.

**Not novel:** Neuroadaptive virtual environments. Numerous groups build EEG-driven VR. NeuroChat (MIT, ACM CUI 2025) already integrates real-time EEG tracking with LLM-driven content adaptation, demonstrating the feasibility of closed-loop cognitive feedback in AI systems. [MIT Media Lab](https://www.media.mit.edu/publications/neurochat-a-neuroadaptive-ai-chatbot-for-customizing-learning-experiences/)

**Partially novel:** LLM-agent civilizational simulation. Project Sid (2024) explored many-agent LLM simulations toward AI civilization, developing specialized roles, governance structures, and institutional dynamics. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.00114)

**Genuinely novel (Contribution 1):** The Transfer Entropy framework for measuring directed human cognitive influence on civilizational structure. No prior work measures $\text{TE}(H \to \mathcal{C})$ across a multi-agent civilization over longitudinal timescales.

**Genuinely novel (Contribution 2):** The susceptibility matrix $\chi$ — the first attempt to formally map which human cognitive dimensions most powerfully couple to which civilizational outcome dimensions.

**Genuinely novel (Contribution 3):** The cognitive climate model — treating a human brain as a low-bandwidth selective pressure rather than a controller. This reframes the entire human-AI interaction problem. Instead of asking how much control a human can exert, we ask how little signal is sufficient to leave a lasting civilizational signature.

**The deepest novelty** is not a method. It is a question that survives the obsolescence of every technology in this stack. The EEG headset will be replaced. The LLM architecture will be replaced. The simulation engine will be replaced. The question — *how much of a mind persists in a society?* — will not.

