## 11. One Sentence That Survives Everything

All models will become obsolete. All hardware will be replaced. All software will be deprecated. The experimental results themselves may be ambiguous in early pilots.

But this question will remain:

> **"We are not asking whether the human enjoys the simulation. We are asking whether the simulation remembers the human."**

And now, for the first time, we have a precise mathematical instrument for answering it: $\mathcal{N}(t) = \text{TE}(H \to \mathcal{C})$.

That instrument — not the VR, not the agents, not the EEG — is the contribution.

Appendices:

Appendix A. Transfer Entropy as a Directed Causal Measure

Definition A.1 (Conditional Transfer Entropy).
Let $H_{0:t} = \{H_0, H_1, \dots, H_t\}$ be the history of the human cognitive state vector up to time $t$, and let $\mathcal{C}_{t+\tau}$ be the civilizational state vector at time $t+\tau$. The transfer entropy from $H$ to $\mathcal{C}$ with lag $\tau$ is

\mathcal{N}_{\tau}(t) = I\bigl(\mathcal{C}_{t+\tau} ; H_{0:t} \mid \mathcal{C}_{0:t}\bigr) = H\bigl(\mathcal{C}_{t+\tau} \mid \mathcal{C}_{0:t}\bigr) - H\bigl(\mathcal{C}_{t+\tau} \mid \mathcal{C}_{0:t}, H_{0:t}\bigr),

where $I(\cdot;\cdot\mid\cdot)$ is conditional mutual information and $H(\cdot\mid\cdot)$ is conditional Shannon entropy.
The neural imprint $\mathcal{N}(t)$ in the main text corresponds to $\mathcal{N}_1(t)$, i.e., $\tau = \Delta t_s$ (the institutional timescale).

Lemma A.1 (Causal selectivity).
If the process $\{\mathcal{C}_t\}$ is conditionally independent of $H_{0:t}$ given its own past, i.e.,

\mathcal{C}_{t+\tau} \perp\!\!\!\perp H_{0:t} \mid \mathcal{C}_{0:t},

then $\mathcal{N}_{\tau}(t)=0$. Conversely, $\mathcal{N}_{\tau}(t)>0$ implies a conditional dependence that cannot be explained by the civilization’s own history alone, which is the operational definition of a directed influence from $H$ to $\mathcal{C}$.

Proof.
The forward direction follows directly from the definition of conditional mutual information: $I(X;Y|Z)=0$ if and only if $X \perp\!\!\!\perp Y \mid Z$.
The reverse direction is the contrapositive: if $I>0$, the conditional independence does not hold, so $H_{0:t}$ provides additional predictive information about $\mathcal{C}_{t+\tau}$ beyond $\mathcal{C}_{0:t}$. This is the standard interpretation of transfer entropy as a non‑parametric, model‑free test for directed statistical influence (Schreiber, 2000; Barnett et al., 2009). ∎

Lemma A.2 (Asymmetry).
In general, $\text{TE}(H \to \mathcal{C}) \neq \text{TE}(\mathcal{C} \to H)$.
Proof. The conditioning sets differ: $\mathcal{C}_{0:t}$ versus $H_{0:t}$, and there is no symmetry requirement. A counterexample with two independent autoregressive processes but unidirectional coupling demonstrates the asymmetry explicitly. ∎

Appendix B. Convergence and Agreement of TE Estimators

Let $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}^{(m)}_{\tau}(t)$ be the estimate of $\mathcal{N}_{\tau}(t)$ obtained from estimator $m$ (Kraskov $k$‑NN, MINE, symbolic TE, directed information). Each estimator is consistent under suitable regularity conditions.

Assumption B.1 (Estimator consistency).
For each $m$, as the number of joint observations $N \to \infty$,

\widehat{\mathcal{N}}^{(m)}_{\tau}(t) \xrightarrow{p} \mathcal{N}_{\tau}(t),

where $\xrightarrow{p}$ denotes convergence in probability.

Lemma B.1 (Convergence‑in‑agreement criterion).
Define the ensemble agreement indicator $\mathcal{A}_\epsilon(t) = \mathbf{1}\Bigl[\max_{i,j} \bigl| \widehat{\mathcal{N}}^{(i)}_{\tau}(t) - \widehat{\mathcal{N}}^{(j)}_{\tau}(t) \bigr| < \epsilon\Bigr]$.
If, for a given session and time $t$, $\mathcal{A}_\epsilon(t)=1$ with $\epsilon$ chosen as twice the maximum theoretical estimator bias at the achieved sample size, then the estimated sign and approximate magnitude of $\mathcal{N}_{\tau}(t)$ are accepted. Otherwise, the result is reported as “estimator‑dependent” and omitted from primary inference.

Proof. Consistency of each estimator guarantees that for any $\delta>0$, there exists $N$ large enough that $\max_m \mathbb{P}(|\widehat{\mathcal{N}}^{(m)}-\mathcal{N}| > \epsilon/2) < \delta$. By the union bound,

\mathbb{P}(\mathcal{A}_\epsilon(t)=0) \le \sum_{i<j} \mathbb{P}(|\widehat{\mathcal{N}}^{(i)} - \widehat{\mathcal{N}}^{(j)}| \ge \epsilon) \le 2 \binom{M}{2} \delta,

with $M=4$. Hence agreement is a high‑probability surrogate for correctness when $N$ is sufficient. The criterion is conservative but ensures that reported neural imprints are not estimator artifacts. ∎

Appendix C. Derivation of the Susceptibility Matrix from the Cognitive Climate Model

The cognitive forcing is $F_H(t) = W \cdot \mathbf{M}_t \in \mathbb{R}^4$. The civilizational response is $R_\mathcal{C}(t) = \nabla_t \mathbf{F}_\mathcal{C}(t)$, where $\mathbf{F}_\mathcal{C}$ is the four‑dimensional fitness vector. At the macroscopic timescale $\Delta t_s$, the evolution of the civilization can be approximated by a first‑order difference equation:

\mathbf{F}_\mathcal{C}(t+\Delta t_s) \approx \mathbf{F}_\mathcal{C}(t) + \Delta t_s \cdot \Phi\bigl(\mathcal{C}_t, F_H(t)\bigr) + \sqrt{\Delta t_s}\, \Sigma(\mathcal{C}_t)\,\boldsymbol{\xi}_t,

where $\Phi$ is a drift function, $\Sigma$ is a noise matrix, and $\boldsymbol{\xi}_t$ is a standard white‑noise vector.

Definition C.1 (Civilizational susceptibility).

\chi_{ij} \equiv \frac{\partial \Phi_i(\mathcal{C}, F_H)}{\partial F_{H,j}}\bigg|_{\mathcal{C}=\text{mean}(\mathcal{C}),\,F_H=0}

evaluated at the neutral forcing point. In the small‑signal regime (the climate analogy), the drift is locally linear: $\Phi(\mathcal{C}, F_H) \approx \Phi_0(\mathcal{C}) + \chi \cdot F_H$, which yields the interpretation: $\chi_{ij}$ is the instantaneous sensitivity of the $i$‑th civilizational outcome to the $j$‑th cognitive dimension.

Lemma C.1 (Relation to the coupling matrix).
If the drift function factorizes as $\Phi(\mathcal{C}, F_H) = A(\mathcal{C}) \cdot F_H$ for some matrix‑valued function $A$, then $\chi = A(\bar{\mathcal{C}})$, where $\bar{\mathcal{C}}$ is the long‑run mean civilization state. Under the linear model, $\chi$ directly inherits the structure of the coupling matrix $W$, but rescaled by the internal dynamics of the civilization. Thus, experimental estimation of $\chi$ provides a quantitative map from specific cognitive states to specific civilizational shifts.

Proof. By definition, $F_H = W \mathbf{M}_t$. Substituting into the drift linearization, the partial derivative with respect to $F_{H,j}$ isolates the $j$‑th column of $A$, so $\chi = A(\bar{\mathcal{C}})$. The matrix $A$ can be recovered from observed trajectories of $\mathbf{F}_\mathcal{C}$ and $F_H$ via least‑squares regression in the main study, provided the forcing is sufficiently rich. ∎

Appendix D. Pareto Frontier Displacement as a Statistical Hypothesis

Let $\mathcal{F}$ be the set of all achievable fitness vectors in a given experimental condition. The Pareto frontier $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{F})$ is the set of points $\mathbf{f} \in \mathcal{F}$ such that no other $\mathbf{f}' \in \mathcal{F}$ dominates $\mathbf{f}$ in all dimensions.

Definition D.1 (Frontier displacement measure).
For two conditions $A$ and $B$, define the Hausdorff distance between their Pareto frontiers:

d_H\bigl(\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{F}_A),\, \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{F}_B)\bigr) = \max\Bigl\{ \sup_{\mathbf{a}\in\mathcal{P}_A} \inf_{\mathbf{b}\in\mathcal{P}_B} \|\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{b}\|,\; \sup_{\mathbf{b}\in\mathcal{P}_B} \inf_{\mathbf{a}\in\mathcal{P}_A} \|\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{b}\| \Bigr\}.

Lemma D.1 (Non‑overlap condition).
If $d_H > 0$, then the set of possible civilizational outcomes differs between conditions; the frontier shapes are not identical. In particular, if the neuroadaptive condition yields $d_H > 0$ relative to all null controls, then the human cognitive climate has shifted the attainable trade‑offs, which is evidence of a genuine causal imprint.

Proof. The Hausdorff distance is a proper metric on the space of compact subsets. A non‑zero distance implies that there exists at least one point in one frontier that cannot be arbitrarily closely approximated by any point in the other, implying a structural difference. Standard multi‑objective optimization theory assures that the Pareto frontier is unique for a given feasible set; therefore, a displacement cannot arise from mere stochastic fluctuation if the distance exceeds a significance threshold determined by bootstrap resampling of trajectories. ∎

Appendix E. Statistical Model for the Neural Imprint Growth Curve

For the pilot study, we model the observed neural imprint $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}(t_k)$ at measurement times $t_k \in \{7,14,21,30\}$ days as:

\widehat{\mathcal{N}}(t_k) = \mu(t_k) + \varepsilon_k, \qquad \varepsilon_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_k^2),

with $\mu(t_k)$ the true imprint trajectory. Under the null hypothesis of no neuroadaptive effect (Conditions A, B, synthetic nulls), we expect $\mu(t_k)$ constant (zero) and $d\mu/dt = 0$. Under Condition C, the cognitive climate hypothesis predicts $d\mu/dt > 0$ on average.

Lemma E.1 (Slope test).
Let $\widehat{\beta} = \frac{\sum_k (t_k - \bar{t})\widehat{\mathcal{N}}(t_k)}{\sum_k (t_k - \bar{t})^2}$ be the least‑squares slope estimate. Under the null, $\widehat{\beta} \sim \mathcal{N}\bigl(0, \sigma^2 / \sum (t_k-\bar{t})^2\bigr)$ asymptotically. Rejecting $H_0: \beta \le 0$ in favor of $\beta>0$ at a pre‑specified significance level provides evidence for a growing neural imprint. The falsification criterion in the main text corresponds to $\beta \le 0$ for Condition C versus $\beta \le 0$ for the null conditions, tested with a one‑sided Welch’s t‑test on the estimated slopes across participants.

Proof. Standard linear model theory. Heteroskedasticity‑consistent standard errors are used to account for possible variance differences across time points. ∎

